WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:05.879
First, let's pop the question, what is photogrammetry?

2
00:00:05.879 --> 00:00:11.119
A quick Google search may give answers such as the science of making reliable measurements

3
00:00:11.119 --> 00:00:15.929
from photographs and especially aerial photographs.

4
00:00:15.929 --> 00:00:21.910
But for us 3D artists, it refers to the process of turning 2D images into 3D models,

5
00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:26.350
an easy way to create a digital twin of a physical model.

6
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:32.049
This also allows you to automatically extract texture maps, which is an added bonus.

7
00:00:32.049 --> 00:00:36.649
Photogrammetry is not the only technology available to create digital twins,

8
00:00:36.649 --> 00:00:39.890
although it is arguably the most popular.

9
00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:44.289
Other techs include LiDAR, also known as laser scanning,

10
00:00:44.289 --> 00:00:50.359
and a newer technology known as NERF, which stands for neural radiance fields.

11
00:00:50.359 --> 00:00:54.679
In this class, though, we'll just be focusing on photogrammetry.

12
00:00:54.679 --> 00:01:00.000
To turn pictures into 3D models, you first need a set of pictures, of course.

13
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.439
How you take those pictures can have a definite impact on the generated 3D model.

14
00:01:05.439 --> 00:01:09.640
The kind of camera you use can also impact the result.

15
00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:12.840
Even though you can theoretically use any camera,

16
00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:17.280
you get the best results with professional DSLR gear.

17
00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:21.920
Try not to use any camera that is less than 16 megapixels in resolution.

18
00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.359
The higher you go, the better.

19
00:01:24.359 --> 00:01:27.920
There is more than just resolution that makes a good camera, though.

20
00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.159
That's why you get the highest quality with DSLR cameras.

21
00:01:32.159 --> 00:01:34.480
Point-and-shoot cameras come next,

22
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:37.879
and smartphone cameras are at the lower end of the spectrum,

23
00:01:37.879 --> 00:01:40.930
although some work rather well.

24
00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:44.370
If you decide to use drones for external shots,

25
00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:48.890
keep in mind that you need a high-quality drone to get any acceptable results.

26
00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:53.530
Many generic drones under $100 boast 4K cameras,

27
00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:55.879
but none of them really works well.

28
00:01:55.879 --> 00:01:58.040
If you're in the market for a drone,

29
00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:02.439
go with an established brand like DJI or similar.

30
00:02:02.439 --> 00:02:04.480
If your camera permits it,

31
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:09.080
shoot in manual mode to keep consistency across your pictures.

32
00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.400
It's important to keep the subject in focus

33
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.509
by ensuring an adequate depth of field.

34
00:02:14.509 --> 00:02:18.780
If you vary the aperture, or f-stop, on your camera,

35
00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:22.750
this will allow you to control the amount of light to use Photoshop,

36
00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:28.229
but a small f-stop value that allows for more light also reduces the depth of field,

37
00:02:28.229 --> 00:02:32.110
potentially making parts of the subject defocused.

38
00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:36.039
The same holds true when varying the focal length.

39
00:02:36.039 --> 00:02:38.599
Given the same f-stop value,

40
00:02:38.599 --> 00:02:46.520
a wide-angle lens will give you a larger depth of field than a telephoto lens.

41
00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:49.159
Avoid changing zoom values on your cameras.

42
00:02:49.159 --> 00:02:51.599
This affects the focal length.

43
00:02:51.599 --> 00:02:55.599
Feel free to move the camera further away or closer to the subject, though.

44
00:02:55.639 --> 00:02:59.159
That would be fine and even recommended.

45
00:02:59.159 --> 00:03:04.669
If possible, use low ISO settings to minimize grain,

46
00:03:04.669 --> 00:03:08.909
and shoot in RAW format if your camera allows it.

47
00:03:08.909 --> 00:03:12.629
Finally, avoid using an automatic white balance

48
00:03:12.629 --> 00:03:16.530
to avoid getting pictures with different color temperatures.

49
00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:21.210
With these basic concepts covered, let's discuss setting the stage.

50
00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:24.729
You can, of course, take pictures indoors or outdoors.

51
00:03:24.729 --> 00:03:28.800
Indoor settings are easier as you can better control the environment.

52
00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:32.520
However, it is usually restricted to small objects.

53
00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:39.419
After all, you'd have a hard time bringing in a boulder or a fire hydrant indoors to photograph.

54
00:03:39.419 --> 00:03:45.099
The idea is to take pictures from all around the subject, in circles or in arcs.

55
00:03:45.099 --> 00:03:49.000
For that, you either physically move around the object,

56
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:54.250
or you use a turntable as you take pictures from different angles and distances.

57
00:03:54.289 --> 00:03:57.409
It's important to move the camera as you take pictures.

58
00:03:57.409 --> 00:04:01.490
Avoid using techniques that you would use for 3D panoramas.

59
00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:06.569
For those, the camera would remain in the same spot as you rotated around.

60
00:04:06.569 --> 00:04:10.240
This does not work for photogrammetry.

61
00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:12.919
Take as many pictures as your patience allows.

62
00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:16.360
The more pictures you take, the better the solution.

63
00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:19.959
For exterior scenes, use a drone for large-scale models,

64
00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:23.089
such as buildings and landmarks.

65
00:04:23.089 --> 00:04:28.730
Speaking of exterior shots, try to do your photoshoot on an overcast day.

66
00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:32.930
This reduces the harsh shadows that take place under direct sunlight.

67
00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:37.060
You wouldn't want those shadows to be baked into the textures.

68
00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:44.139
For interior shots, you can eliminate or at least drastically reduce shadows using soft-diffuse lighting.

69
00:04:44.139 --> 00:04:46.579
There are such solutions for every budget,

70
00:04:46.579 --> 00:04:54.430
from commercial photography soft-light box rigs to dollar store ring-led lights.

71
00:04:54.430 --> 00:05:01.560
Ultimately, and for best practices, you may decide to equalize the pictures you've taken for consistency.

72
00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:04.480
There are various applications that allow you to do that.

73
00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:08.589
Some are commercial, while others are open-source.

74
00:05:08.589 --> 00:05:12.129
I've listed a few on this slide for your convenience.

75
00:05:12.129 --> 00:05:18.410
In the next video, we'll discuss some of the limitations that you will undoubtedly face when dealing with photogrammetry.
